{
  "dom_id": "step-q330910",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-2.25<Z<2.25).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0096\\,$ | $\\,0.0094\\,$ | $\\,0.0091\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0125\\,$ | $\\,0.0122\\,$ | $\\,0.0119\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ | $\\,0.0154\\,$ |\n\n$P(-2.25<Z<2.25)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-2.25<Z<2.25).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/6d34a217c29edd92527e2c31a954af6e631755c178ecacc797ed6c0c5da0f25b.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.25<Z<2.25) & =P(Z<2.25)-P(Z\\le -2.25) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.25)-P(Z\\le -2.25) \\\\  & =Φ(2.25)-Φ(-2.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.25)=0.0122,\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $Φ(2.25).$ However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.25) & =P(Z\\le 2.25) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -2.25) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.25) \\\\  & =1-0.0122 \\\\  & =0.9878\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.25<Z<2.25) & =Φ(2.25)-Φ(-2.25) \\\\  & =0.9878-0.0122 \\\\  & =0.9756.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
